  "notes": "West leads the ♠10 against 6NT. There are 10 top tricks (3 spades, 3 hearts, 1 diamond, and 3 clubs). The diamond finesse could provide South with an extra trick and it should be possible for South to develop at least one extra trick in clubs.\n\nAfter winning West's spade opening lead, South should finesse East for the ♦K before he touches the club suit. When the diamond finesse wins, South's total is up to 11 tricks. As only 1 extra club trick is now required, South should start the suit by leading toward dummy's ♣10. This is a safety play against West having 5 clubs headed by the ♣J.\n\nOn the actual layout, West will win the ♣J. South can win any return that West makes, unblock the ♣10, and easily win the rest of the tricks. If the diamond finesse had lost to the ♦K with West, South would need 2 extra tricks from clubs and he would know to play that suit from the top, hoping for a 3-3 break.\n\nThe correct play in clubs depends on whether South needs 4 or 5 tricks from that suit. Taking the diamond finesse first is a discovery play that will let South know how many clubs tricks are required (and how that suit should be played).",
